groups for example the famous uh so-called hot stuff club which Cedric would make

00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:56.920
mysterious

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.360
references to and it was never entirely clear whether the club was simply in

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.500
Cedric's mind or there's a sort of faint illusion on one occasion that it was a

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.700
women-only club and it was a sort of Cedric would have lunch with his coven

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.560
but it may have been entirely invented so what was Cedric like well he was

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:22.160
first off he was funny I mean he was witty but it was more than that because

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:28.226
because he could do, I would call them caricatures rather than impersonations of

00:01:28.226 --> 00:01:29.060
people, the

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:35.998
voices, the mannerisms. And my favourite example of this was an account he gave, I

00:01:35.998 --> 00:01:36.760
mean he'd

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:46.648
been there, some aristocrats castle, a weekend sort of party, and I think it was

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:47.760
probably,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.680
it was somebody like the Duke of Chumley if that's not a made-up name but it was

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.660
the man who used to walk backwards at the opening of Parliament this this this

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:03.720
this custom has now been abandoned anyway two of the guests were Buckminster

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.120
Fuller and Baron Philippe de Rothschild who took an instant dislike to

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.580
Buckminster Fuller who described him as a windbag and they had a great falling

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:19.260
out an argument about something or other. Now Cedric's account of this involved him

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.792
doing this caricature of both Buckminster Fuller and Baron Philippe de Rothschild

00:02:23.792 --> 00:02:23.960
and

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.343
reference to the Duke who used to walk out backwards at the Queen's opening of

00:02:27.343 --> 00:02:27.820
Parliament.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:32.857
It was a wonderful, wonderful party piece but I only saw him do it once so perhaps

00:02:32.857 --> 00:02:32.980
it

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:39.880
wasn't a party piece. So he was funny and witty and scabrous. I mean Cedric, people

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:45.986
used to tell Cedric things in the way that partly because you knew that he wouldn't

00:02:45.986 --> 00:02:46.280
land

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.827
you in it by citing you as the author of some piece of gossip or other, but partly

00:02:51.827 --> 00:02:52.300
because

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.236
he was a good gossip himself. I mean, good gossips have to exchange. I mean, they

00:02:57.236 --> 00:02:57.480
have

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.903
to provide as well as receive, which he always did. And the gossip could be about

00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:02.340
anything

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:08.953
and everything, politics, you know, a bit about acting sometimes, though he never

00:03:08.953 --> 00:03:09.280
told

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:17.122
Eleanor Bronze stories. Eleanor's view about the sort of Cedric's coterie I think

00:03:17.122 --> 00:03:17.800
was one

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.849
of interest but not necessarily enthusiasm. For example, she would usually put in a

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:24.140
very

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:32.440
brief appearance at the Christmas lunch and then make a sort of mock funny actressy

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:32.740
bow

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:38.060
bow out to sort of leave us to it.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:46.707
Cedric's take on life involved aphorisms and he could sort of think in headlines or

00:03:46.707 --> 00:03:47.540
captions

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.020
and he could sum up situations just very wittily.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:59.138
For example, there was a competition to design a new telephone kiosk for London and

00:03:59.138 --> 00:03:59.920
the sort

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.380
proposers that were coming through, shall we say they were rather bland in terms of

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.633
materials and colour compared with the traditional Giles Gilbert Scott telephone

00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:10.880
kiosk. And Cedric's

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:19.164
comment on this, yes a pity about those old telephone kiosks, they stood out a mile.

00:04:19.164 --> 00:04:19.460
And

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:24.395
of course that's exactly what they were supposed to do and it's exactly what the new

00:04:24.395 --> 00:04:25.100
ones didn't.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:32.100
And he liked sort of reversal, he liked kind of reversal jokes.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:38.175
So for instance, at one time he did a column for Building Design where it was sort

00:04:38.175 --> 00:04:38.620
of ask

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:39.780
Cedric Price.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.423
He was an agony aunt and people would write in with what their problem was and

00:04:44.423 --> 00:04:44.780
Cedric

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:51.040
would give an entirely non-serious and often provocative or satirical answer.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.740
But of course no one ever did write in.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.740
Cedric invented all the questions himself and they would come from people

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:02.180
who would only know who they were from their initials. But of course if you knew

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.060
Cedric and you knew sort of public life generally you could often work out what

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.500
he was getting at and of course by partly anonymizing who the question was

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:16.620
coming from you know he could be wonderfully scabrous and rude in his

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:22.020
response without actually being directly insulting to a particular individual. The

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:28.460
The one time this actually didn't work was there used to be a comic strip in Britain

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:35.900
in the 1950s called PC49, Police Constable 49.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:42.938
And Cedric decided to send in a question from PC49 who would be on duty outside

00:05:42.938 --> 00:05:43.740
number 10

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:44.740
Downing Street.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.680
So the question was of a political nature.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.480
Unfortunately, we had a New Zealand sub-editor who'd never heard of PC-49.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:59.040
So he was absolutely not supposed to touch anything that Cedric had written.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:03.180
I was the only person who was supposed to edit it or have anything to do with it.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:08.094
But on this occasion, the New Zealand sub-editor, because he thought that obviously

00:06:08.094 --> 00:06:10.580
Cedric and I were incompetent grammarians,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.860
decided that what was required was a comma.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:20.360
So instead of PC49, it became PC, 49 Downing Street.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:24.860
So the misuse was funny in itself.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:29.140
And you have to say that Cedric would be very frustrated

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.860
by any sort of error or problem

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.080
with any material he supplied for publication.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.220
But it was almost as though,

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.680
because he was slightly kind of paranoid about it,

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.920
it almost attracted trouble.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:49.600
and the number of times when a drawing would allegedly go missing or not be returned

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:55.980
or on one occasion a whole pasted up double page spread of material he supplied

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:01.600
got trapped in the typesetter's photocopying machine and was semi-destroyed

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:07.183
and Cedric would be furious but he would also always see the funny side at the end

00:07:07.183 --> 00:07:07.480
of it

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:14.300
And in exactly the same way, for somebody who kind of theorised interaction

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:18.860
and in a sense the digital world 30 years before it happened,

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:25.981
it was always very funny to see the delight that he took in a sort of W.C. Fields

00:07:25.981 --> 00:07:26.220
way

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:34.880
in trying to cope with simple stage props like the hard-boiled egg slicer

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:38.660
or a preposterous air conditioning machine he had

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:42.980
which ran on batteries and clockwork and had to be filled up with water.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:48.400
And Sedgwick had this kit in his office, including the baby bedding thing

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.060
where he would occasionally cook breakfast.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:55.480
And it was like a professor out of the 19th century

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.480
meddling with mechanical objects with great delight

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:01.680
and sort of enjoying doing it,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.630
even though what he was actually thinking about was, you know, probably 20 years

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:06.420
into the future.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.440
Ah, well, Cedric's architecture, I think, is an interesting story all of itself

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:17.434
because, I mean, Cedric used to complain that nobody put him on lists to do

00:08:17.434 --> 00:08:18.100
buildings,

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.420
certainly not in the last 20 years of his professional career.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:25.867
But actually, you know, I think the truth is he wasn't very interested in actually

00:08:25.867 --> 00:08:26.800
constructing things.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:28.280
He was interested in ideas.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:33.080
and although you know people like Steve Mullin who did work on Sedgwick

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:36.320
buildings in the early years of the office and he did do buildings but I

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.300
think increasingly that there was a sort of reluctance to engage in the idea of

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:45.400
actually building anything so for example he was brought in to design a

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:50.720
greenhouse at Parc Lavillette sort of via Bernard Schumi but in particular

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:56.880
there was a French client and Sedgwick was on you know some sort of top fee

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.380
rate which was greater than anyone I mean it was you couldn't get any higher

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.580
than this sort of triple a fee rate for doing this job and it was a typical it

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:11.640
was fantastic would have been a fantastic Cedric project it was glass it

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:17.120
was structure he got in Frank Newby to work on it as the engineer but the near

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.420
of this thing came to possibly being built it seemed to me the more nervous

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:28.740
Cedric became about it and actually in the end he told me with sort of mock

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:35.220
horror but I detected an underlying sense of relief that he was not going to

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:40.860
be able to continue with these people as he called them because they decided he

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.300
would now the job was about to proceed they could take him off the triple a fee

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.560
scale and just go on to a sort of double a fee scale which was still more than

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.640
most architects were getting for any work for the French state.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.980
But this act of Les Majestés towards Cedric gave him the perfect excuse,

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.420
saying that he couldn't rely on them.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:02.340
They were messing him about on fees,

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.800
and it was time for him to withdraw from the project.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:08.680
Now, it may have been that the project was in trouble, for all I know,

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:12.000
but I think, honestly, that he took it as a relief,

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.020
that he'd found a way of getting out of it.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:15.640
I don't think he was afraid of building.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.620
I think he just wasn't very interested in it.

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:23.685
I mean, what he was interested in was ideas, the ideas behind the building, the

00:10:23.685 --> 00:10:29.520
actual business of running a contract and having, you know, working details.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.920
And actually, all the people who worked on the interaction project in Camden will

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.680
tell you it was pretty shambolic.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.467
I mean, it was not really what you would expect from a professional architectural

00:10:40.467 --> 00:10:40.900
operation.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:45.285
Cedric left it up to these untried kids in the office, like, you know, Will also had

00:10:45.285 --> 00:10:47.060
a bit more experience than others.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.740
but Paul Hyatt, I think, was doing his year out.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.660
He certainly didn't know what he was doing, and we'll tell you that himself.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.880
And they were sort of bodging together details.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:00.180
And actually, I think his heart wasn't really in it.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:04.280
I think he proved with the Snowdon aviary

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:09.020
that he was perfectly capable of working in an interesting way

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:11.720
on interesting architecture and getting it built.

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:15.680
And actually, the more structural things were, I think the more he liked it.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:21.388
I mean, I think in a sense is, you know, the Fun Palace idea is in the end, it's an

00:11:21.388 --> 00:11:27.028
idea about structure and servicing and an idea of architecture rather than an idea

00:11:27.028 --> 00:11:29.160
about how you build a building.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:36.000
So, you know, in the same way, he had an attitude to the temporary.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:42.370
He always claimed that by the time people wanted to save a building, it was time for

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:42.960
it to go.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.058
And I once rang him up, and this was obviously before the internet and where you

00:11:48.058 --> 00:11:52.200
could still get scoops and find things out the old-fashioned way,

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:58.142
and I found out that his aviary in Regent's Park was about to be listed as being a

00:11:58.142 --> 00:12:01.700
building of historic and or architectural interest.

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:06.220
and I knew that Cedric's principles would tell him

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:09.940
that that was not what he would have wanted

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:14.280
but any architect who has a building listed in their own lifetime,

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.220
I mean, you're allowed a little bit of personal pride, aren't you?

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.920
Anyway, I rang him up and it was Cedric Price

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:23.920
and I said, Cedric, I've got some really terrible news.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:25.160
He said, what's happened? What's happened?

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.580
I said, they've listed your aviary.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.060
There was a long pause and he said,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:39.080
dreadful dreadful but I could hear in his voice actually he was rather pleased

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:45.300
but so I think he didn't have any huge affection for the the messiness of

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:50.300
building and that side of things and he was just far more interested in the kind

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:54.780
of intellectual processes of why you'll be wanting to do a building in the first

00:12:54.780 --> 00:13:03.524
place. As a sketch idea, Bat Hat is just one that is very strongly in my mind. Of

00:13:03.524 --> 00:13:04.280
course,

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:11.917
the more serious projects, one would have to say the Potteries Thinkbelt. Towards

00:13:11.917 --> 00:13:12.200
the

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:16.908
end of his career, he did a great project called Magnet, which we published in the

00:13:16.908 --> 00:13:17.540
Architect's

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:21.854
Journal and which was the subject of an exhibition at the Architecture Foundation

00:13:21.854 --> 00:13:22.440
when it was

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:27.660
still in the Economist building in Berry Street and I remember going to a Saturday

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:33.900
afternoon seminar where the comment was made about well where do you find

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:40.200
avant-garde architecture in London the answer is here with this at that time

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:45.660
relatively elderly architect actually but the ideas that he would would that

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:50.920
he was espousing you know the connection between things across the city and the

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:54.460
way you might either physically or psychologically make those sort of

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:59.380
connections. I think was it was an extremely interesting one and really the

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:10.600
last significant exhibition he did. I met Cedric I think in the 1970s when he

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:15.340
wrote a column, I mean he wrote columns for Building Design magazine at different

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:20.080
periods and the columns are always different in tone and he knew the then

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:25.195
editor Peter Murray and we were introduced at some event or other and then our paths

00:14:25.195 --> 00:14:25.560
didn't

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:32.744
really cross until I myself became editor of building design in 1983 and I bumped

00:14:32.744 --> 00:14:33.720
into Cedric

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:39.344
I think it was at the building centre opposite his own office at some event or other

00:14:39.344 --> 00:14:40.080
and he said

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:46.594
well come to the office let's have breakfast and that was the start of a 20-year

00:14:46.594 --> 00:14:48.060
relationship based

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:56.174
generally speaking round a formula. It was an eight o'clock meeting. He would almost

00:14:56.174 --> 00:14:57.140
invariably

00:14:57.140 --> 00:15:02.461
be there earlier than that and was quite grumpy if for some reason he was late. He

00:15:02.461 --> 00:15:03.240
would supply

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:08.261
orange juice and coffee and brandy. I would supply cheese and tomato rolls with

00:15:08.261 --> 00:15:08.960
poppy seeds

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:16.180
from Sidolis, the cafe that was on the ground floor of his own building.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:21.680
And as often as not, he would have a sort of informal agenda,

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:25.520
things he wanted to talk about, and he would have newspaper cuttings.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.920
He'd have got books from his collection

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:33.000
opened at various pages that he wanted to refer to.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:38.480
And we would have a mixture of news and gossip and things to talk about.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:43.340
And for me, I mean, it was because, I mean, I'd studied history at university.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.420
I'd never been to architecture school.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:49.040
Everything I knew about architecture had come from really being a reporter

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.060
and then an editor on an architectural weekly.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:55.960
So I picked up a lot, but I didn't have a formal background.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:57.280
I think Cedric liked that.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:01.480
I mean, Cedric wasn't wild about architects

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.700
or the structures of architectural education

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:11.700
as an appropriate formation for anyone in particular.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:16.660
So I think in a sense, I was somebody who had a lot to learn,

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:21.040
and I did learn a lot from Sedgwick, but it was an absorption method.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:23.880
I mean, he didn't give lectures on this and that.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:25.420
You would just talk about stuff.

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:31.140
I think Sedgwick had a critical position in architectural education,

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:37.980
partly because although never a full-time teacher nor did he build very

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:43.980
much so he was difficult to categorize and I think therefore as a as a as a

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:48.780
curiosity in the architectural profession he would be referred to as

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:52.320
the conscience of the profession precisely because he didn't get engaged

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:56.520
in the messy business of actually having to build things and make decisions about

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:00.120
clients he could stand aloof from all that and I think that is quite

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:05.120
attractive actually for architectural students who know that there are people

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:10.880
in the profession who don't operate by its normal set of mores and they don't

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:15.100
operate like you know the full-time academics in the schools that in short

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:19.800
they're free spirits and that's why you would get a huge crowds of students when

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:24.480
he would give his famous lectures that might go on for two three four hours or

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:28.860
you know when the red wine or the brandy ran out and he was always immensely

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:33.501
popular partly because he was a good presenter and he was funny but partly because

00:17:33.501 --> 00:17:34.180
he held this

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:41.633
sort of position of being in a sense in a sense above it all or aside from it all he

00:17:41.633 --> 00:17:42.520
was always

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:47.348
there but he wasn't necessarily part of the gang he influenced Archie Graham but he

00:17:47.348 --> 00:17:48.220
wasn't a member

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:53.758
of Archie Graham he taught at the AA but he wasn't an AA teacher and so on and so

00:17:53.758 --> 00:17:54.100
forth

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:59.000
So in a sense, you know, he was the cultivated maverick.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:04.928
I think Cedric's influence as an architect depended on the quality of the ideas

00:18:04.928 --> 00:18:08.380
which he pursued through a series of projects,

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:16.201
Potteries Thinkbelt, Fun Palace in particular, which were addressing not just a

00:18:16.201 --> 00:18:20.540
formal problem or a formal potential commission,

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:26.724
but we're addressing a whole series of other things that were happening in society

00:18:26.724 --> 00:18:27.780
at that period

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:35.371
which might be susceptible to some sort of architectural either solution or at the

00:18:35.371 --> 00:18:36.760
least analysis.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:42.574
So in the case of the Fun Palace for instance, I mean that's dealing with ideas

00:18:42.574 --> 00:18:43.420
about leisure,

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:54.180
social mobility, the role of a public building in at least a private public world,

00:18:54.920 --> 00:19:02.343
the notion of mingling, the notion of indeterminacy is neither one thing nor the

00:19:02.343 --> 00:19:02.900
other.

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:09.520
Cedric's ideas about how you would think about buildings and their location.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:15.158
So at a simple level the mental map which says that one place is nearer to another

00:19:15.158 --> 00:19:20.520
place because you can reach it by rail, not because of geographical proximity.

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:25.663
Which sounds a bit of a commonplace now but it wasn't when he was talking about that

00:19:25.663 --> 00:19:26.520
sort of thing.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:30.020
He would make connections between things not because they were next to each other

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:33.520
geographically at all but because they were next to each other for other reasons.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:39.880
reasons. The whole notion of things being one thing rather than another as opposed

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:46.360
to being indeterminate or vague or fuzzy was another thing that I think intrigued

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:51.820
people. I mean he would he would come out with things he would he would drop into

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:57.400
some article or conversation the difference between if I'm remembering

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:05.873
this in the right order, tolerance, fit and calculated inaccuracy. Now people kind

00:20:05.873 --> 00:20:06.080
of

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:10.718
like that because in a world where everything was supposed to be kind of squeaky

00:20:10.718 --> 00:20:11.240
clean and

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:16.200
defined right down to the last detail, to have somebody coming along and say well it

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:23.240
never is actually. There are a few messier things than the manufacturer of all those

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:29.941
sort of cold metallic elements that look so crystalline in buildings and actually

00:20:29.941 --> 00:20:30.520
they're

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:39.857
all hideously energy consuming and actually made often by convicts in conditions of

00:20:39.857 --> 00:20:41.320
extreme heat.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:47.632
And then Caryl Cedric was into all those ironies and ambiguities and I think people

00:20:47.632 --> 00:20:48.440
kind of spot

00:20:48.440 --> 00:20:56.023
that in the work. And I think the projects like the Potteries Thinkbelt, which was,

00:20:56.023 --> 00:20:56.480
well,

00:20:56.480 --> 00:21:01.792
in a sense, of course, was tied in to a 19th century idea, which was the importance

00:21:01.792 --> 00:21:01.920
of

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:05.190
the railways. And there was a period in which you think, well, Cedric missed the

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:05.680
boat because

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:10.180
actually it's all motorways these days. But hey, guess what? Railways have made a

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:10.680
comeback.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:14.742
So all that stuff that he did about why can't you have a university that runs on

00:21:14.742 --> 00:21:15.300
rail tracks

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:16.860
rather than being in fixed buildings.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:21.680
Well, you know, the whole thing, his idea about polyarch,

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:24.580
which was how systems of architectural education

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:26.300
should relate to modern technology,

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:29.740
i.e. why can't all architecture students in the world

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:31.980
get the best structural lecture in the world

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:33.220
from the best structural engineer?

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:35.500
Why do they have to hear from 100 different ones

00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:37.300
when they could all hear from the best?

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:40.000
You know, what is the difference

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:41.960
between centralisation and devolution?

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.300
How can you actually exploit

00:21:44.300 --> 00:21:47.640
what he was always interested in conceptually,

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:50.980
computerisation, digitisation.

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:53.100
You know, this was the man who couldn't really use

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:55.040
a photocopying machine without help.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:57.740
But in his mind,

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.780
he was kind of already there with this stuff.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:04.160
It didn't really matter if you could use a photocopier

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:05.020
or that wasn't the point.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:07.580
I mean, actually, photocopies are pretty old-fashioned things

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.000
by today's standards.

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:13.220
But it was what he was thinking about conceptually,

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:18.260
and I think that's what made him of abiding interest,

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:25.140
plus the writing and a very substantial archive of projects that no one knew about.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:28.560
I mean, it's only recently we discovered just how much work he did

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:31.620
with a British rail client on King's Cross.

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:36.220
So, you know, Cedric kept certain things close to his chest,

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:39.940
plenty of projects he never talked about, or he certainly didn't talk about to me,

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:44.080
even though he talked about other conceptual projects.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:47.420
For example, he was always absolutely intrigued.

00:22:47.500 --> 00:22:48.940
He was the first person to tell me

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:50.980
that one of the biggest holes in Europe

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.960
would be built in Stratford,

00:22:54.400 --> 00:22:57.380
which was, of course, the relief roof of fire exits

00:22:57.380 --> 00:22:58.840
for the Channel Tunnel railing.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:00.880
He told me this years and years before

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.000
anybody knew anything about it.

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:04.860
He loved Stratford, of course.

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:05.980
That's another story.

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:14.094
Cedric had an original mind. He didn't like banging on about the same thing over and

00:23:14.094 --> 00:23:14.480
over

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:20.166
again. There were one or two stories he would tell. His obscure remark, which I can

00:23:20.166 --> 00:23:20.920
never quote

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:25.098
accurately, it was something to do with the invention of electricity and its effect

00:23:25.098 --> 00:23:25.400
on the

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:31.810
people who used to make candles. He used to sort of half jokingly let you believe

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:32.760
that Price's

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:37.252
candles with some sort of immensely rich family company. It had absolutely nothing

00:23:37.252 --> 00:23:37.800
to do with

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:44.030
him whatsoever. I mean, it's rather like he would sometimes say, oh, it's only rich

00:23:44.030 --> 00:23:44.480
people

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:49.015
like me who can afford to live in Harley Street. Well, I don't think one could ever

00:23:49.015 --> 00:23:49.780
have described

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:56.906
Cedric as truly rich. He was rich in mind and spirit, most certainly. But he had a

00:23:56.906 --> 00:23:57.340
great

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:04.571
phrase which is if he did repeat something his phrase to sort of describe why he was

00:24:04.571 --> 00:24:05.260
doing it

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:12.097
was necessary repetition for the willfully inattentive and this was the typical sort

00:24:12.097 --> 00:24:12.260
of

00:24:12.260 --> 00:24:18.492
Sedgwick phrase which was sort of slightly ticking people off if they weren't paying

00:24:18.492 --> 00:24:19.160
attention

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:24.105
and also justifying something he would normally not do which was repeat himself. So

00:24:24.105 --> 00:24:24.820
I think what

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:33.980
he was always looking for was not, I don't think he was a neophiliac. I mean, no one

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:42.640
only interested in the New could possibly have relied on pretty archaic systems of,

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:49.640
let's say, his bookshelf, the way he kept books. It was very old-fashioned in a way.

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:57.110
It was unique to him of course. My life with Cedric was based around breakfasts and

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:57.560
being

00:24:57.560 --> 00:25:03.316
the occasional editor of columns that he wrote, first for Building Design,

00:25:03.316 --> 00:25:04.560
subsequently for

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:11.017
the Architect's Journal when I changed magazines in 1994. So apart from the

00:25:11.017 --> 00:25:12.480
breakfast, he was

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:18.374
also a contributor to magazines I was editing. And the columns were always very

00:25:18.374 --> 00:25:19.120
different,

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:21.480
They were different in format, different in subject matter.

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:25.100
The final series that he did for the Architect's Journal

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:28.000
were really a series of collages with captions

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:33.220
of things that had caught his eye during the week or the month

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:38.640
that he put together with some kind of political and or architectural comment.

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:44.580
And I suppose that what I benefited from

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:49.100
in this relationship of editor-contributor

00:25:49.100 --> 00:25:52.220
was a certain way of thinking about things.

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:56.220
And I think it enhanced...

00:25:57.280 --> 00:25:59.040
Well, I think I had a bit already,

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.140
which was a certain ability to look at situations

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:07.060
and to think clearly about them, you know, as a historian might.

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:11.640
But to come at things from slightly different directions,

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:15.720
to think, actually, we're thinking about this in a certain way,

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:18.140
but are we thinking about it the right way round?

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:22.500
was this event or that movement or that style,

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:24.860
was it actually the start of something?

00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:27.800
Maybe we should think about it as the end of something else.

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:31.060
And I think those sorts of perspectives,

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:35.280
precisely at what angle are you taking

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:37.740
in coming to any sort of analysis,

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:41.860
let alone conclusion, about, I mean, is it really a problem?

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:45.340
You know, the apocryphal story of,

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:49.911
and it may well have been Cedric saying to the couple, the squabbling couple who

00:26:49.911 --> 00:26:50.940
wanted a new house

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:53.940
and his professional advice to them was to get a divorce.

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.180
Why waste money on a house?

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:02.100
Now, even if Cedric didn't originate that, I mean, who knows?

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:06.559
But the point is that it's commonly ascribed to him because it illustrates something

00:27:06.559 --> 00:27:07.920
about his way of thinking.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:11.860
And in a way, his sort of thing, well, if a building is...

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:13.120
Now, this is one of his things.

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:17.880
he said to me, if a building is the answer, what was the question?

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:23.299
Example, he was rung up by the BBC and asked if he would like to take part in the

00:27:23.299 --> 00:27:23.880
competition,

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:31.027
early stage, you know, long list, to replace, do a new broadcasting house on Langham

00:27:31.027 --> 00:27:31.500
Place.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:34.940
The building subsequently became the Langham Hilton.

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:39.045
But anyway, at the time, the BBC owned it, and they were thinking about a new

00:27:39.045 --> 00:27:39.860
broadcasting house.

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:45.430
And Cedric said, well, I'd only be interested in joining your long list of

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:51.000
architects if you'd be interested in what I think you should have more of.

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:52.720
And they said, well, what is that?

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:54.560
And he said, radio cars.

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:56.900
And there was a long silence.

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:58.840
And of course, he didn't go on the long list.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.040
He didn't have any interest in going on the long list.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:02.000
He thought it was ludicrous.

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:04.060
He didn't think they needed a new building.

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:08.055
He thought in the age of digital technology when everybody can be their own

00:28:08.055 --> 00:28:09.120
cameraman as it were

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:13.242
You know, what do you need a building for? Who's it for? It's not for broadcasters.

00:28:13.242 --> 00:28:14.700
It's actually for a bureaucracy

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:19.872
And that that sort of Cedric way of either reversing something or coming at it from

00:28:19.872 --> 00:28:21.360
a completely different angle

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:24.480
Came naturally to him. It wasn't an affectation

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:29.902
Didn't I don't think he went around thinking now, how can I think differently about

00:28:29.902 --> 00:28:33.200
this problem? He would automatically do it. Well Cedric's

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:39.580
Cedric's notion of delight, I think really was commodity, firmness and delight.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:46.252
I think in his view, the worst aspects of dreary functionalist architecture was

00:28:46.252 --> 00:28:48.960
that, you know, where was the joy?

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:51.140
Cedric liked delight.

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:56.540
You know, he liked the sort of kind of weird office cricket matches.

00:28:57.720 --> 00:29:01.380
He liked handmade Christmas cards.

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:09.880
he loved sketching, he was convivial, he was fun, he loved food and drink.

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:16.725
He loved cheap cuts as well as expensive ones, bath chaps, you know, obscure cut of

00:29:16.725 --> 00:29:17.880
the pig's head

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:19.680
that you would get at the Christmas parties.

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:27.380
You know, he was almost perversely traditionalist in some of the things he liked,

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:31.932
but that was because he really enjoyed them and he really liked them and I think he

00:29:31.932 --> 00:29:32.480
was always

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:38.865
looking for some uh some some evidence that there was more to life than simply

00:29:38.865 --> 00:29:40.420
producing efficient

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:45.054
envelopes I mean efficient envelopes have their place but I mean not they weren't

00:29:45.054 --> 00:29:45.740
not actually

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:50.646
that interesting apart from people who are interested in efficient envelopes um and

00:29:50.646 --> 00:29:51.060
I think

00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:57.180
therefore that he that was why any any meeting with Cedric you know any

00:29:57.180 --> 00:30:05.520
breakfast always had that potential for fun and amusement. Now by contrast fun

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:10.780
had to be treated with great discretion because Cedric had this horror of what he

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:15.960
called compulsory fun or indeed compulsory delight and the point about

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:22.140
it was that you got your fun or you delight by being a fun person or a

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:27.400
delightful person or doing things in a certain way not by having it as a

00:30:27.400 --> 00:30:32.680
tick-box item on your agenda. Must inject fun at this point would have been the

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:38.800
most ghastly thing you could possibly have imagined. I think Cedric Price has

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:44.320
relevance today firstly because lots of people are still interested in what he

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:52.780
had to say and the proof of this is in the series of publications, exhibitions,

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:59.200
the Venice Biennale in 2014, the Swiss Pavilion and I think the interest of

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:03.940
younger generation of architects in finding out about who exactly this man

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:08.680
was. I mean there isn't a huge built legacy, there are some things. I think

00:31:08.680 --> 00:31:14.760
Cedric's ideas are still, many of them are still completely relevant to today.

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:20.200
I mean I was looking at a project recently for a major global organisation

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:25.000
that's commissioned a new headquarters and is doing huge amount of research and

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:30.580
analysis into what a 21st century headquarters might be for the many

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:36.120
people who would work there or interact with it in some way. And actually the

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:43.500
images of the building, the film of how it would operate. Actually I suddenly

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:47.700
realized after about 10 seconds I'm looking at the Fun Palace. This is the

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:53.760
building that's infinitely adaptable, infinitely flexible, can be fashioned

00:31:53.760 --> 00:32:00.300
both to individual use and to collective use with people even having even going a

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:03.660
bit further than the Fun Palace in some ways personal transportation systems

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:09.121
inside the building. But the thinking struck me as well. You know, this is pure

00:32:09.121 --> 00:32:10.020
Cedric Price.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:18.147
You know, it's four, five decades old. And that's fine as well. I mean, it's not a

00:32:18.147 --> 00:32:19.140
question of,

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:23.644
well, it's not a question of people plagiarising Cedric's ideas. People who have

00:32:23.644 --> 00:32:24.320
great ideas,

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:28.999
in the end, I think, are flattered by the fact that their ideas have influence and

00:32:28.999 --> 00:32:29.420
they are

00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:35.600
picked up by others. They are part of a sea in which we all swim and I don't

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:39.380
think Cedric would have any problem at all about seeing a project that

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:45.260
clearly owed things to his lines of thinking. He wasn't in a vacuum, he'd been

00:32:45.260 --> 00:32:49.860
taught by people like Arthur Corn at the Architecture Association. He himself came

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:54.440
from a certain tradition of thinking about things in certain ways and from

00:32:54.440 --> 00:32:58.980
that point of view I would take some sort of comfort from the fact that in a

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:04.638
sense the continuity that he represented in the history of architectural ideas is a

00:33:04.638 --> 00:33:05.320
continuity

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:11.911
which is alive and well and I suspect that because it's based on ideas and the

00:33:11.911 --> 00:33:12.840
imagination

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:19.485
rather than as it might have been if Cedric had been more profoundly interested in

00:33:19.485 --> 00:33:20.620
actually doing

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:25.744
buildings and becoming a wealthy practitioner that actually it would have been a

00:33:25.744 --> 00:33:26.320
different

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:31.266
history but the fact that it isn't about construction and buildings and building up

00:33:31.266 --> 00:33:32.100
a sort of mega

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:37.529
practice means that I think there's far more likelihood that the ideas will survive

00:33:37.529 --> 00:33:37.660
as

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:40.940
the product of a unique individual mind.
